if you watched my first xbox series x  preview i covered backwards  compatibility and performance  low times and quick resume and have a  few more details on that  but i have a lot more to discuss now  including series x specific games and  enhancements  external storage options and how well  they work and more on the overall next  gen experience  keep in mind that this is not a review  rather a more detailed look at our  series x preview units  so let's kick things off with talking  about some real next-gen games  i've had access to early builds of a few  series x specific games such as yakuza  like a dragon and dirt 5  and the series x optimized versions of  gears tactics and gears 5.  as a big time yakuza fan leica dragon is  one of my most anticipated games of the  year  and this demo filled me with so much joy  and excitement but instead of waxing  poetic about how lovely and wild it is  let's look at its next-gen performance  you get three enhancement options here  high resolution gives you full native 4k  resolution but runs around 30 fps  normal gives you 1440p resolution with a  consistent 60fps  high framerate does 1080p also add 60fps  but negates potential frame drops if  they come up  i mostly play on normal and didn't think  twice about switching because for me  it's the best balance it looks great and  maintains high fps  so there's really no need to drop the  visual quality further for the high  frame rate mode  leica dragon's yokohama is a wonderfully  detailed setting and the best use of the  yakuza series dragon engine  which was used in kiwami 2 yakuza 6 and  judgment  it looks and runs great  dirt 5 also presents 3 enhancement  options image quality aims for full 4k  while using dynamic resolution to try to  maintain  60fps but it seemed to me that there  were some frame drops when particle  effects kicked up while a bunch of cars  were on screen  frame rate mode also uses dynamic  resolution but lacks some visual flair  reducing effects like shadow quality to  maintain a more consistent 60fps  if you have a 120hz display and set the  system output to 120hz  dirt 5 will offer the 120hz option  as a pc gamer who's been on high refresh  rate monitors for quite some time  it's a real treat to see a console game  hit 120 fps  sure the graphical quality takes a hit  but in return the game moves buttery  smooth  the visual compromises are quite  apparent to me but i wouldn't say it  makes the game look bad  overall though i do feel image quality  is the best option here  both yakuza like a dragon and dirt 5 are  cross gen games  so while they might not blow you away  with the next gen wow factor  there are measurable graphical  improvements over what we'd expect from  the xbox one x  with gears tactics it keeps things  simple on the xbox series x the game  runs at 4k  60fps no options or compromises  necessary  this is in contrast to the xbox one x  where you have the option for 4k  30fps with quality mode and 1440p 60fps  with performance mode  the bump in frame rate is nice but this  being a turn based strategy game doesn't  have that much of an effect on the  experience  certainly not as much as it would on a  racing game such as dirt 5  or a shooter like gears 5. so with gears  5 you get a new option with the xbox  series x  120 fps in multiplayer of course this  only matters if your display can do 120  hertz  4k or otherwise and from what i can tell  the jump in frame rate is a great touch  to make the action noticeably smoother  the bump won't affect the single player  campaign though it will run a dynamic 4k  at 60fps like the one x but with a few  perks like improved anisotropic  filtering and more consistent  performance and image quality due to the  stronger hardware  performance in backwards compatible  games really depends on how they're  built  what their technical limitations are and  whether or not they've been updated with  series x optimizations  i mentioned this in my first preview  that final fantasy xv for example  runs at a much more consistent fps on  series x when set to its performance  mode whereas on the one x  you'll experience some noticeable frame  drops however using the higher quality  mode still caps the game at 30 fps on  both systems  since the game is built with that frame  cap when running high fidelity modes  on that note other games that cap the  frame rate at 60fps will be able to  actually hit that high mark  and or maintain it more consistently  than the xbox one  as made evident in games such as monster  hunter world sekiro shadows died twice  no man's sky and plenty more but a game  like the outer worlds which caps at  30fps and doesn't have graphics  options will still run at 30fps on  either system  more recently i cracked into doom  eternal the game maintains consistent  performance with 60fps but runs a  dynamic resolution that maxes out at  1800p on the xbox one x  and turns it down when things get hectic  on the xbox series x  it's able to keep resolution at or  closer to 1800p  since the hardware is powerful enough to  do this and still hit 60fps  so in short if a game uses dynamic  resolution or allows higher fps  you'll get improvements by virtue of the  more powerful series x hardware  even if there aren't series x  optimizations if a game is bound by  certain technical limitations such as a  30 fps cap or a fixed resolution  the game will have to adhere to that  accordingly it just depends on how the  game is built  of course it's possible that some games  could also get updates for sears x  optimizations in the future  so keep in mind that these conditions  can change one such case is destiny 2  which caps at 30 fps for now  but will improve to 60fps when its next  gen update rolls around  load times are one of the biggest  improvements you get with the xbox  series x  the console really leverages that solid  state drive as you'll see  in our results so this is where you're  gonna get that practical benefit that  permeates the entire experience  we previously used four games to test  load time improvements and to quickly  summarize  we got the following results on one x  and series x when loading a save  from the title screen red dead  redemption 2 went from 2 minutes and 8  seconds  down to 38 seconds control went from 58  seconds  to 10 seconds final fantasy 15 went from  1 minute and 11 seconds to 13 seconds  and the outer worlds went from 43  seconds to 11 seconds  we did a similar comparison with gears 5  and gears tactics  both of which are cross-gen games with  series x optimizations  gears 5 went from 28 seconds on the 1x  to just  5 seconds on the series x to load into  act 1 mission 3  and gear's tactics improved greatly as  well going from 52 seconds  to 18 seconds to load into a mission  when it comes to ongoing multiplayer  games you're constantly going back to  you'll definitely be saving some time  take destiny 2 and its current state for  example on 1x  getting from sign in to character select  took a minute and 34 seconds  and loading from orbit to the trostlin  edz took 1 minute and 12 seconds  on the series x the game took about 18  seconds and 21 seconds  for those respective scenarios while  yakuza like a dragon and dirt 5 will be  available on xbox one we didn't have  access to those versions for this  preview  but to give you an idea of what to  expect on series x  loading a save in yakuza took just 6  seconds  and hopping into career mode race in  dirt 5 took about 12 seconds  the time you save adds up in both games  since you're frequently loading into  different scenarios and scenes in yakuza  and entering races in dirt 5. after  having spent a bunch of time on the xbox  series x the differences are night and  day which makes it hard to go back to  the hard drives of old  on xbox one and ps4 the xbox series x  comes with a one terabyte  internal ssd which actually comes out to  802 gigabytes of free storage you get to  use  and with the size of games today that  might fill up fast so you have a few  options in terms of how you can bulk up  your storage now there are some  important details that you'll need to  consider  so first let's start with the one  terabyte seagate ssd that slots right  into the console  this is technically the best solution  since it's identical to the internal ssd  there's virtually no difference in load  times in our testing using the games i  previously mentioned  it's built specifically to interface  with the xbox hardware  and comes with all the same benefits  like running series x and s  specific games without a hitch however  it carries a hefty price tag at  220 bucks the other solution is to get  an external usb  3.0 drive but things can get a little  tricky here  you'll be able to play backwards  compatible xbox one and previous gen  games  straight off the drive but not series x  optimized games  you can still use it to store series x  games but to play them  you'll need to transfer them to the  internal drive or expansion card if you  have one  this can also apply to certain xbox one  games that have received series x  optimizations as was the case in gears 5  and gears tactics  i tested this out using a samsung t5  external ssd  and a usb 3.1 cable and in terms of  moving data around  and loading the results were very very  impressive  the outer worlds is a 45 gigabyte game  and took 2 minutes and 54 seconds  to move to the external ssd and when  loading into the same save as a previous  test  it only took 13 seconds which is  extremely close to the internal ssd  speeds  final fantasy 15 is an 84 gigabyte file  and took longer to transfer  at 5 minutes and 50 seconds but the load  time results were great  just taking 15 seconds with the external  ssd  of course both are xbox one games  without series x optimizations so the  results between internal  and external storage come from the fact  that the games are working off of ssd  speeds  but since external usb ssds can only  play xbox one games anyway  and can still do quick resume this is a  great solution for them  especially when something like the  samsung t5 is more cost effective at  around 110 bucks for 500 gigabytes  and 140 bucks for one terabyte now  you're probably wondering how the xbox  series x has revamped the ui  but let's just say that nothing has  really changed at all at least  not for now  [Music]  now what you see here is not the final  interface again this is all in a preview  state and is subject to change somewhat  by launch  the cold boot up is quite fast as you  see here and navigating the interface is  much snappier than it has been in xbox's  past  and a cold boot up of games themselves  is of course faster as well a big note  on the quick resume feature though  all games that have been suspended  through quick resume will retain their  suspended state  if you turn off the console and even if  you unplug the console too  i did encounter a few rare bugs where  games like yakuza dirt 5 and gears 5  froze when trying to jump back in thus  requiring a full reboot of the game  we should expect these hiccups to be  worked out in the system's final build  however  i do think it's really nice to have 5 to  6 games simultaneously suspended and  be able to jump between them in a matter  of 8 ish seconds  without loading anything else even if  you don't juggle that many games  this feature still benefits the handful  of games you keep coming back to  and it's not like you have a choice to  use it quick resume is just built into  the way the series x works  i do wish there was some sort of  notification that let you know when  you're pushing the limits and have to  close a game's suspended state though  i think we've exhausted all the  conversations around the xbox series x's  brick like design  and we've seen it from all different  angles and we've seen it stacked against  other consoles  but a big topic that has come up is heat  yes the xbox series x gets hot  the top half of the console gets quite  warm to the touch and the air that's  blown out from the top exhaust is hot as  well  as is the case with most high-end  computers i haven't had any issues with  overheating but you should be mindful of  giving the series x's top exhaust enough  room if you're fitting it in a tight  space  on the bright side though the system  runs whisper quiet even under heavy load  keep in mind that this is a non-final  version of the system  so thermal management could  theoretically change with all that said  i'm sure someone out there has measured  the exact operating temperatures of the  system but i'm gonna hold off until we  get the final retail build  so there it is our last extensive  preview of the xbox series x  before it launches on november 10th so  far we've seen measurable performance  and graphical improvements with series x  optimized games  even if they may not blow you away with  backwards compatibility the boosts we  see now are genuinely exciting and will  continue to be  as more games get proper enhancements of  course fast load times keep on  impressing and although there were some  hiccups that occurred i'm still a fan of  quick resumes functionality  i've tried to highlight the aspects of  next-gen that i think matter most  but i can't cover every little detail  all in one go  so if you have any remaining questions  feel free to hit me up in the comments  below or on twitter at michaelp heim and  i will do my best to try and answer them  in detail on our weekly show  jen next thank you all for watching and  i'll see you all  next time  you

